(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["neural-humoral"]="<h1>Neural and Hormonal Control</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-body-need-central-control\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Why does the body need central control?</h2>\n<p>Local control looks after <strong>one tissue at a time</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation\">→ Local Regulation</a>)</em> But someone has to look after the <strong>whole circulation</strong>: keeping arterial pressure high enough to perfuse every organ, and moving blood to where it's needed.</p>\n<p>That central control works on the variables you already know <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/blood-pressure\">→ Blood Pressure</a>)</em>:</p>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"map\">Mean arterial pressure</span> ≈ cardiac output × total peripheral resistance</strong>, and <strong>cardiac output = heart rate × <span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">stroke volume</span></strong>.</p>\n<p>So the nervous system and hormones can raise blood pressure in three ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Speed up the heart</strong> (heart rate).</li>\n<li><strong>Strengthen each beat</strong> (stroke volume, via contractility and <span data-term=\"venous-return\">venous return</span>).</li>\n<li><strong>Narrow the arterioles</strong> (peripheral resistance).</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-control-centre-in-the-medulla\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> The control centre in the medulla</h2>\n<p>The heart and blood vessels are controlled by groups of neurons in the <strong>medulla oblongata</strong>, together called the <strong>vasomotor centre</strong>. It has two opposing parts:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Pressor area</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Depressor area</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Where</td>\n  <td>Upper, lateral part of the medulla</td>\n  <td>Lower, inner part of the medulla</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Does</td>\n  <td><strong>Excites</strong> the sympathetic neurons in the spinal cord</td>\n  <td><strong>Inhibits</strong> the pressor area</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Result</td>\n  <td>Blood pressure <strong>rises</strong></td>\n  <td>Blood pressure <strong>falls</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The pressor area sends fibres <strong>straight down to the sympathetic neurons in the spinal cord</strong>. Nearby are the <strong>vagal neurons</strong> that slow the heart. Sensory signals from the body's pressure and chemical sensors arrive at a relay station, the <strong>nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)</strong>, which adjusts all of these. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/reflexes\">→ Reflexes</a>)</em></p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture's \"pressor\" and \"depressor\" areas correspond to what modern texts call the rostral ventrolateral medulla (pressor) and the caudal ventrolateral medulla (depressor).</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"3-sympathetic-control-of-the-blood-vessels\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Sympathetic control of the blood vessels</h2>\n<p>Sympathetic fibres supply the smooth muscle of <strong>all blood vessels except the capillaries</strong> (and the smallest venules, which have little muscle). Most release <strong>noradrenaline</strong>, which acts on <strong>α₁ receptors</strong> to <strong>constrict</strong> the vessel.</p>\n<p>They do two different jobs, depending on the vessel:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Vessel</th>\n  <th>What constriction does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Arterioles</strong> (resistance vessels)</td>\n  <td>Raises <strong>resistance</strong>, so <strong>blood pressure rises</strong> and flow is redistributed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Veins</strong> (capacitance vessels)</td>\n  <td><strong>Squeezes stored blood</strong> out of the veins towards the heart, raising <strong>venous return</strong> and so cardiac output</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>The key idea: a constant background &quot;tone&quot;</h3>\n<p>The sympathetic constrictor fibres fire <strong>all the time</strong>, at a low rate. This <strong>tonic discharge</strong> keeps every arteriole partly constricted. There is <strong>no</strong> equivalent background firing in dilator fibres.</p>\n<p>So the body controls vessel width by <strong>turning the sympathetic tone up or down</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>More</strong> sympathetic firing → <strong>constriction</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Less</strong> sympathetic firing → <strong>dilation</strong>. No dilator nerve is needed; the vessel simply relaxes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That is why cutting the sympathetic nerves (or a spinal injury) makes vessels dilate and blood pressure fall.</p>\n<p><strong>An exception: sympathetic cholinergic dilator fibres.</strong> Some sympathetic fibres to skeletal muscle release <strong>acetylcholine</strong> and <strong>dilate</strong> the vessels. They are switched on by the cortex, in <strong>anticipation</strong> of exercise or in alarm.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> These cholinergic dilator fibres are well shown in animals; their importance in humans is uncertain. Human sympathetic control of blood vessels is mainly noradrenergic and constrictor.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-control-of-the-heart-sympathetic-and-vagus\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Control of the heart: sympathetic and vagus</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Sympathetic</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Parasympathetic (vagus)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Transmitter and receptor</td>\n  <td>Noradrenaline on <strong>β₁</strong></td>\n  <td>Acetylcholine on <strong>M₂</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Heart rate</td>\n  <td><strong>Faster</strong> (<span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span>)</td>\n  <td><strong>Slower</strong> (SA node)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span></td>\n  <td>Faster conduction</td>\n  <td>Slower conduction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Force of contraction</td>\n  <td><strong>Stronger</strong></td>\n  <td>Little effect on the ventricles</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction\">→ Cardiac Conduction</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>At rest, the vagus is in charge.</strong> The SA node on its own would fire at about 100 beats/min. Constant <strong><span data-term=\"vagal-tone\">vagal tone</span></strong> holds the resting rate down to about 70. So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cutting the vagus</strong> (vagotomy) → heart rate <strong>rises</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Atropine</strong>, which blocks the vagus's muscarinic receptors → heart rate <strong>rises</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The vagus acts mainly on the heart, not the vessels.</strong> Most blood vessels have little or no parasympathetic supply. So the vagus controls blood pressure mainly through <strong>heart rate</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–4\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–4 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–4</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> How do the sympathetic nerves make an arteriole dilate?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They release acetylcholine onto every arteriole in the body</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They increase their firing, which opens the arteriole wider</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. They switch on a separate set of tonic dilator fibres</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They reduce their background constrictor firing, so the muscle relaxes</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Sympathetic constrictor fibres fire tonically. Turning that tone down is how most vessels dilate.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A patient is given atropine. What happens to the resting heart rate, and why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It rises, because atropine blocks the vagal brake on the SA node</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It falls, because atropine blocks sympathetic β₁ receptors on the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It rises, because atropine directly stimulates the SA node cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It stays the same, because the vagus has no effect at rest</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> At rest, vagal tone holds the rate below the SA node's own rate. Removing it lets the rate rise.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> What does sympathetic constriction of the veins achieve?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It raises peripheral resistance, the main cause of high pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It reduces the volume of blood returning to the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It pushes stored blood towards the heart, raising venous return</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It makes new blood to replace volume lost in a bleed</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Veins are the blood reservoir. Squeezing them raises venous return and so cardiac output.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"5-hormones-that-act-on-the-circulation\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Hormones that act on the circulation</h2>\n<p>Hormones act more slowly than nerves, but their effects can last much longer.</p>\n<p><strong>Constrictors and volume-raisers:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Hormone</th>\n  <th>From</th>\n  <th>Main effects</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Adrenaline</strong></td>\n  <td>Adrenal medulla</td>\n  <td><strong>β₁:</strong> faster, stronger heart. <strong>β₂:</strong> dilates arterioles in skeletal muscle and liver. <strong>α₁</strong> (at high levels): constricts most other vessels.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Angiotensin II</strong></td>\n  <td>Made in the blood when the kidney releases <strong>renin</strong> (low pressure)</td>\n  <td><strong>Constricts</strong> arterioles; triggers <strong>aldosterone</strong>, <strong>thirst</strong> and <strong>ADH</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Aldosterone</strong></td>\n  <td>Adrenal cortex</td>\n  <td>Kidneys keep <strong>Na⁺</strong> (and water follows), raising blood volume over <strong>hours to days</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>ADH (vasopressin)</strong></td>\n  <td>Posterior pituitary</td>\n  <td>Kidneys keep <strong>water</strong>; at high levels, also <strong>constricts</strong> arterioles</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Dilators</strong> (from the lecture):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Substance</th>\n  <th>Main effects</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>ANP</strong> (atrial natriuretic peptide)</td>\n  <td>Released when the atria are <strong>stretched</strong> by too much volume. <strong>Dilates</strong> vessels and makes the kidneys <strong>lose Na⁺ and water</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Kinins</strong> (e.g. <span data-term=\"bradykinin\">bradykinin</span>)</td>\n  <td>Made in the plasma and tissues. <strong>Dilate</strong> arterioles, through endothelial NO.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>VIP</strong> (vasoactive intestinal polypeptide)</td>\n  <td><strong>Dilates</strong> vessels, especially in the gut</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why can adrenaline both dilate and constrict?</strong> Because different vessels carry different receptors. Muscle arterioles have many <strong>β₂</strong> receptors, which dilate; skin and gut arterioles have mainly <strong>α₁</strong> receptors, which constrict. So adrenaline <strong>redirects</strong> blood towards the muscles.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-fast-slow-and-slower-putting-it-together\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> Fast, slow and slower: putting it together</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Speed</th>\n  <th>Mechanism</th>\n  <th>Good for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Seconds</strong></td>\n  <td>Nerves (sympathetic, vagus) and the reflexes that drive them</td>\n  <td>Standing up, sudden blood loss, exercise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Minutes</strong></td>\n  <td>Adrenaline, angiotensin II, <span data-term=\"adh\">ADH</span></td>\n  <td>Sustaining the response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Hours to days</strong></td>\n  <td><span data-term=\"aldosterone\">Aldosterone</span>, ADH and the <strong>kidneys' control of salt and water</strong></td>\n  <td>Restoring <strong>blood volume</strong>; long-term blood pressure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Haemorrhage, step by step:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Blood volume falls, so venous return, <span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">stroke volume</span> and arterial pressure fall.</li>\n<li><strong>Within seconds:</strong> sympathetic firing rises and <span data-term=\"vagal-tone\">vagal tone</span> falls. The heart beats <strong>faster and harder</strong>, arterioles <strong>constrict</strong>, and veins <strong>squeeze</strong> stored blood towards the heart.</li>\n<li><strong>Within minutes:</strong> adrenaline, angiotensin II and ADH reinforce the constriction.</li>\n<li><strong>Over hours to days:</strong> aldosterone and ADH make the kidneys keep salt and water, and thirst rises, slowly <strong>refilling</strong> the circulation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The nerves can <strong>hold up the pressure</strong>, but they can't replace lost blood. Only the slower, kidney-driven steps restore the volume. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 5–6\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 5–6 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 5–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why does adrenaline increase blood flow to skeletal muscle while reducing it to the skin?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Muscle arterioles have α₁ receptors that dilate; skin arterioles have β₂ that constrict</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Muscle arterioles have β₂ receptors that dilate; skin arterioles have α₁ that constrict</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Adrenaline reaches muscle only, because skin has no blood supply from the aorta</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Adrenaline acts only on the heart, and the muscle simply receives the extra output</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The response depends on the receptors each vessel carries.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> What stimulates the release of ANP, and what does it do?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Low blood pressure; it constricts vessels and makes the kidneys keep salt</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Low blood volume; it triggers thirst and the release of aldosterone</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Atrial stretch; it dilates vessels and makes the kidneys lose salt and water</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Sympathetic firing; it speeds the heart and strengthens its contraction</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> ANP is the body's response to too much volume: it lowers volume and pressure.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> After a large haemorrhage, which mechanism actually restores the lost blood volume?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Sympathetic constriction of the arterioles throughout the body</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Kidney salt and water retention driven by aldosterone and ADH</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Sympathetic constriction of the veins that store the blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A faster heart rate driven by the withdrawal of vagal tone</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Nerves raise pressure within seconds but don't add volume. The kidneys, over hours to days, do.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"7-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>MAP ≈ CO × TPR</strong>, and <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>. Central control adjusts heart rate, contractility, venous return and arteriolar tone.</li>\n<li><strong>Vasomotor centre</strong> in the medulla: the <strong>pressor area</strong> excites spinal sympathetic neurons; the <strong>depressor area</strong> inhibits it. Sensory input arrives at the <strong>NTS</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sympathetic constrictor fibres</strong> (noradrenaline, α₁) supply all vessels except capillaries. <strong>Arterioles:</strong> resistance. <strong>Veins:</strong> push stored blood to the heart.</li>\n<li><strong>Tonic constrictor discharge:</strong> less firing = dilation. There is no tonic dilator discharge.</li>\n<li><strong>Sympathetic cholinergic dilators</strong> supply skeletal muscle (doubtful importance in humans).</li>\n<li><strong>Heart:</strong> sympathetic (β₁) = faster and stronger; vagus (M₂) = slower. <strong>Resting vagal tone</strong> holds the rate down, so vagotomy or atropine raises it.</li>\n<li><strong>Hormones:</strong> adrenaline (β₁, β₂ dilation in muscle, α₁ constriction elsewhere), angiotensin II, aldosterone, ADH raise pressure or volume; <strong>ANP, kinins and VIP dilate</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Timescales:</strong> nerves in seconds, hormones in minutes, <strong>kidneys over hours to days</strong>. Only the kidneys restore lost volume.</li>\n</ol>\n";
